      dek: 'The Housing Ministry is buying emergency housing modules, but the tender is contracted by a trust whose trustee is Conafin AFISA, of the National Development Corporation.',
      contexto: 'The 2023 census identified 67,024 households with critical housing deficiencies in Uruguay: 1,447 with walls or roofs made of waste material, 2,895 with dirt floors, 9,447 without an exclusive bathroom and 9,041 without adequate sanitation. In February 2026 the Ministry of Housing and Territorial Planning announced call 03/2025 for the supply and installation of up to 300 housing modules across the country, intended to address housing emergencies: habitable modules comprising living-kitchen, bedroom and bathroom (standard and accessible), plus an integral one-bedroom module, along with accessories such as connectors, stairs, accessible ramps and wastewater tanks.',
      hallazgo: 'The ministry does not process this call through the usual state procurement channel: the contracting party is the Social and Urban Integration Trust (FISU), whose trustee is Conafin AFISA of the National Development Corporation (CND), with the MVOT, the Agriculture Ministry and the Ministry of Economy as settlors, and it is published on the CND\'s tender portal. Bids opened on 23 February 2026 at 11:00. The specifications require non-traditional construction systems with a current MVOT technical approval (DAT or CIR), a bank credit line of at least 1,000,000 pesos proportional to the declared production capacity, ten-year durability, and manufacture within 30 calendar days of the contract being finalised. The award goes to the lowest total-cost bid, with multiple awards possible and minimum lots of ten modules. Minister Tamara Paseyro said "this population needs us today more than any other, because 31% of children under six live in multidimensional poverty".',
      statusNote: 'The ministry announced the call on 3 February 2026 and bid reception closed on 23 February 2026. The initiative falls within the ministry\'s Five-Year Housing and Habitat Plan. As of this file\'s closing (August 2026) there is no public information on the award or the final contract amount.',
      porQueImporta: 'An emergency module can be the difference between a dirt floor and a floor, today. But buying them through a trust means the unit price, the supplier and the quantity actually delivered do not end up in the same public record as the rest of the State\'s purchases.',
